ha256hash":"fc15c4be9d5f7216c27f4010cefb2e53a3dff524d7cd825f7a5dc72ccbec5b1a","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/fc15c4be9d5f7216c27f4010cefb2e53a3dff524d7cd825f7a5dc72ccbec5b1a","scanners":{"vt":null,"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":760,"checkedAt":1789202782942},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This EasyEDA skill is purpose-aligned but opens an unauthenticated local bridge that can run arbitrary EasyEDA code against connected projects.","dimensions":[{"detail":"The bridge binds to 127.0.0.1, but it uses a predictable port range, wildcard CORS, no WebSocket Origin validation, no authentication token, and no operation allowlist, so any local process or reachable browser context may be able to send execution requests.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The package has a simple npm dependency on ws and no install hooks; setup is disclosed, including installing an EasyEDA-side Run API Gateway extension and, in one guide, installing this skill into an AI tool config only after user consent.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The trigger list includes broad terms like EasyEDA, PCB, EDA, schematic, and circuit board, while the runtime instructions start a background bridge and execute code through /execute; this makes accidental activation more plausible for a high-impact capability.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"There is no system-level persistence or autostart, but the instructions run the bridge as a background process and only ask about stopping it after topic changes, leaving a powerful local execution interface open during the session.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill coherently provides EasyEDA API references, extension guidance, and live debugging, but the shipped bridge forwards arbitrary JavaScript to a connected EasyEDA client and can therefore read, modify, or delete sensitive design/project data.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Use this only if you intentionally want an AI agent to control a running EasyEDA session. Start the bridge only while needed, close it afterward, avoid browsing untrusted sites while it runs, and treat connected EasyEDA projects as accessible to local software until the bridge adds authentication and origin restrictions.","findings":"[T09] unexpected: Arbitrary EasyEDA code execution is part of the stated purpose, but the lack of authentication, origin checks, and scoped commands is not proportionate for a bridge that can affect active design data.\n[SQP-1] unexpected: The broad activation terms are real and matter because they can load instructions that start or use the bridge for high-impact EDA operations.\n[AE1] expected: The referenced quick-reference file exists locally and is a large API index; the scanner's partial-inspection note appears to reflect file size rather than hidden or unavailable payloads.\n[P6] expected: The cited 'print rule' content is PCB design-rule documentation, not a system prompt or instruction to reveal hidden prompts.\n[PE3] expected: The .npmrc references are troubleshooting guidance for a project-level npm registry setting and explicitly warn not to modify global npm configuration without consent; they are not credential access.\n[P2] expected: The cited hidden-comment markers are empty Markdown/HTML artifacts around generated API reference text, with no concealed directives found in the inspected examples.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789202782942}}}}}